  "honest_assessment": [
    "The Var(d_FR) ~ 1/K scaling on the K-simplex is mathematically guaranteed by concentration of measure on the sphere S^{K-1}. This is NOT an empirical discovery -- it is a theorem. The experiment confirms the numerical constant.",
    "The real question is whether geodesic variance gives USEFUL K estimates for real systems (models, nuclei). This requires: (a) the system's states actually live on a simplex, and (b) the observed distribution of states is approximately uniform on that simplex. Neither condition is guaranteed.",
    "N=27 models is far too small to estimate geodesic variance reliably on ANY simplex. With 27 points on the K-simplex, pairwise distance statistics are dominated by sampling noise for K > ~5. Group-level K estimates (10 base, 17 aligned) are even noisier.",
    "The nuclear cross-check normalizes (Z, A, Q_alpha) to the 3-simplex. This is a convenient mapping but has no physical justification. The nuclear state space is NOT the probability simplex. Any correlation found may reflect trivial Z-A-Q relationships rather than K-dependence.",
    "NOVEL FINDING (if any): The concentration-of-measure constant c in Var = c/K on the probability simplex with Dirichlet(1,...,1) sampling appears to be close to pi^2/4 (the full-sphere value), but slightly lower due to the positive-orthant constraint. The exact constant for the probability simplex is not widely tabulated and may be useful.",
    "LIMITATION: This approach requires observing MANY independent draws from the K-simplex to estimate variance. For a single system with fixed K, you need many (O, R, alpha) measurements under different conditions. For the AI rating agency, this means scoring the SAME platform many times under varied conditions -- which is effectively what longitudinal monitoring does."
